1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
of humanizing people, makes people feel worse

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,200
about treating them badly.

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:07,600
All right, so other questions?

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,000
Questions, all right.

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,560
So moving on to other games.

6
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:14,760
So now let's move on to the not as famous

7
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,400
but also pretty famous coordination game.

8
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,600
It's got a high profile in theory and policy discussion.

9
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:22,240
So here's a pretty standard representation.

10
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:24,340
So we can either move left or right.

11
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:27,000
Left or right.

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,840
Okay.

13
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:34,320
And if we both move left, then we both get an okay payoff.

14
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:36,680
We both move right, we both get a good payoff.

15
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:39,400
And if we do different things, then we both get nothing.

16
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,040
All right, so that's the structure of a coordination game.

17
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:47,080
If we do different things, then we get bad payoffs.

18
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:49,400
So we wanna do the same thing.

19
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,520
But some same things are better for both of us

20
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:54,380
than other same things.

21
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:55,380
So that's the idea.

22
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:58,380
All right, so the natural solution concept here

23
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:00,460
is pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

24
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:01,540
Where if you just take a look,

25
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,260
then obviously this one's gonna be

26
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:05,300
a pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

27
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:06,660
Right, this one is better than anything.

28
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:08,560
So of course that's going to be one.

29
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:10,380
But if you look at it, this one is a pure strategy

30
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:12,280
Nash equilibrium as well.

31
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,940
Because given that the other person is playing left,

32
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:16,920
you wanna switch over to right?

33
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:18,980
No, because then you both get zero.

34
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:20,680
Right, so you don't wanna do that.

35
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:24,220
All right, now the most obvious case of this

36
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:27,180
is literally which side of the road to drive on.

37
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:31,260
So presumably based upon human biology,

38
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:32,380
how around the world it's normal

39
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:33,820
for people to be right-handed.

40
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:37,180
So presumably there actually is one better side of the road

41
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:39,280
that's safer for human beings in general.

42
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,780
But if your country's riding on the left side of the road,

43
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,060
suppose that it's the right side of the road.

44
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:46,260
If everyone else in your country's driving

45
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:47,100
on the left side of the road,

46
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:48,820
do you wanna be the pioneer who says,

47
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,820
I'm gonna start driving on the other side of the road?

48
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:53,780
Probably, you probably don't.

49
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:55,100
Have you ever seen the pictures from Sweden

50
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:56,260
when that happens?

51
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:57,420
No, actually, yes.

52
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:00,100
There's, they did that in Sweden during the 1960s

53
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:01,940
and there's, even if you look it up on Wikipedia,

54
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:03,540
there's a funny picture of it.

55
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:05,340
Were there any deaths?

56
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:10,300
Yes, yeah, it was like a long transitory period

57
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,780
where they were training people up for it.

58
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:14,820
Do you remember hearing about that?

59
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:16,180
It was just like a top-down,

60
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:18,260
you know, hey, we're switching from the left to the right.

61
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:21,060
So for this period of time, watch out.

62
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:22,820
I don't understand.

63
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:25,020
All right, now this coordination,

64
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:28,140
the approved coordination underlies a large

65
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:31,980
and still hot policy area.

66
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:35,580
So this is often called the path dependence literature.

67
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,140
All right, all right, and the main idea here

68
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:40,660
is that it is possible for people to be locked

69
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:43,340
into a Pareto inferior equilibria,

70
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,580
or to be locked into a Pareto inferior equilibrium,

71
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,900
usually in the context of technology.

72
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:52,940
Usually in the context of technology.

73
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:56,020
The idea is that there could be a bunch of technologies,

74
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:58,140
some are going one to one to the best,

75
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:00,500
but if we start off with the wrong technology,

76
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:02,180
then people will just stay with that one

77
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,100
because nobody wants to be the only person

78
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:06,500
using some weird technology that nobody else uses.

79
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,340
Beta-max.

80
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:08,260
Yes, beta-max.

81
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:10,900
So the most famous example is,

82
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:14,100
which probably is barely,

83
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:16,580
you'll see, you guys have watched a videotape at some point,

84
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:18,220
have you like put a videocassette

85
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:19,580
into a machine at some point?

86
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:20,420
We had beta-max.

87
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:22,180
Yes, all right.

88
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:24,740
So anyway, back when I was a kid,

89
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:27,180
there was a great format war between VHS,

90
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:29,220
which won, and beta-max.

91
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:31,020
All right, for a very long time,

92
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:32,820
there were people saying that beta-max should have won,

93
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:36,180
it deserved to win, and was the superior technology.

94
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:37,860
And then there was a story saying

95
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:39,500
that the problem was path dependence.

96
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,540
VHS came on first, and then just locked in,

97
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:44,260
and then no one wanted to do something different.

98
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:46,700
This is a deeply factually wrong story

99
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:48,540
because beta-max came first.

100
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:51,100
VHS won anyway, right?

101
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:53,460
And then you're like, wow, that's kind of weird.

102
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:56,620
So it was worse, and it still won,

103
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:57,740
even though it came second.

104
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,540
It was worse and late, and it won.

105
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:02,500
There's actually a pretty,

106
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:04,980
while there were some clear advantages for beta-max,

107
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:07,460
so it gave you better picture quality, things like that,

108
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:10,340
but there also was a clear advantage for VHS,

109
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:12,900
which is you could fit an entire movie on one tape.

110
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:17,380
Now, clearly, every human being on Earth

111
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:19,180
values picture quality over having to get up

112
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:21,220
in the middle of a movie and switch tapes, right?

113
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:26,220
No, actually, there's a whole unwashed,

114
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:28,020
all the unwashed masses might sit there

115
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:30,580
and not even care about what the picture quality looks like,

116
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:32,100
but they don't want to have to get up out of their chair

117
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:33,140
and change the tape.

118
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:34,420
So that's another explanation,

119
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:38,380
was that VHS actually was the better technology

120
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:41,460
given the preferences of the philistines

121
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:42,820
that inhabit a narrow town,

122
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:46,380
or whatever story you want to tell.

123
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:49,180
You also remember the televisions back then were so bad,

124
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:51,180
I know it's unclear what that was a thing,

125
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:54,220
what VCR, what kind of VCR you had,

126
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:55,900
the televisions were terrible too.

127
00:04:57,220 --> 00:04:59,580
Any case, so, but as you can see here,

128
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:01,580
it's possible to get locked in there.

129
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:04,260
Now, possibility is very different from proof,

130
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:05,860
and this is the thing to watch

131
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:08,500
whenever you're reading empirical applications

132
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:11,380
of this concept, because very often,

133
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:14,380
people who don't will go and start applying the model

134
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:16,100
before they know the facts, right?

135
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:18,700
I think we were gonna talk about some other example,

136
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:22,140
we'll talk about some other factual examples as we go on,

137
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:25,580
but there's two economists that are well known at GMU,

138
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:27,700
Margolis and Leibovitz, who have gone through

139
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,540
and debunked a bunch of very popular examples

140
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:32,980
of path dependence.

141
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:34,260
Probably the one they're most famous for

142
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:35,420
is the QWERTY keyboard.

143
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:39,860
So, now, so the anyway, so you know how,

144
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:42,420
if you just visualize typing Q,

145
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:45,340
Q-W-E-R-T-Y on a keyboard,

146
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:46,660
you're basically going to the second row

147
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:50,740
and just typing letters in order from left to right.

148
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:52,340
All right, now there's a standard story

149
00:05:52,340 --> 00:05:54,780
about why keyboards look this way,

150
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:57,380
and the story goes back when we had regular typewriters

151
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:59,140
with physical keys.

152
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,620
Anyone ever typed on a regular physical typewriter

153
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:02,820
with keys?

154
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:03,980
Yes.

155
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:06,500
So you had to actually, the goal was to,

156
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:08,820
supposedly was to slow things down a bit,

157
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,180
be to avoid jamming keys.

158
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:14,380
All right, and then, supposedly,

159
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,380
there was an Admiral Dvorsak who went and came

160
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:18,780
with a new keyboard, which was actually better.

161
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:19,860
And it was supposedly even better

162
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:22,460
even with the problem of jamming of keys.

163
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:25,020
Anyway, so, and this story's been told repeatedly,

164
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:26,460
and then, of course, once you had computers

165
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:28,900
and computer keyboards, those keyboards don't jam

166
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:31,140
in the same way physical ones jam, right?

167
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:32,860
So then it would seem like it would make a lot of sense

168
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:35,780
to switch to a new and better keyboard configuration.

169
00:06:35,780 --> 00:06:36,740
Like when you first learn to type,

170
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:39,900
do you ever ask someone why isn't it just alphabetical?

171
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:42,500
That's another possible way of doing it.

172
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:45,860
Anyway, according to Magrose Leibowitz,

173
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:47,700
the real story is that these early experiments

174
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:50,060
demonstrating the superiority of the Dvorsak keyboard

175
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:52,940
were actually done by Admiral Dvorsak himself.

176
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:54,540
And so we shouldn't put a lot of weight

177
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:56,260
in these experiments, given that the guy

178
00:06:56,260 --> 00:06:58,020
is running and testing his own ideas.

179
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:00,100
There was one study that was his relative, actually.

180
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:02,020
But that was pretty much it, one study,

181
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:03,540
and it was the guy's relative.

182
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:05,980
Oh, his relative, and then, just through the game

183
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:07,860
of telephone, he's repeated over and over.

184
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:09,180
Now, here's the other interesting thing,

185
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:11,860
is that now it is actually very easy to go

186
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:14,020
and download new drivers so that you can transform

187
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:17,380
a QWERTY keyboard into any keyboard you want.

188
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:19,340
Anyone, has anyone done this?

189
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:21,660
I've tried it once with a Dvorsak keyboard.

190
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:23,980
And how'd it work out?

191
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:25,500
Well, you did it only once, I guess.

192
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,540
I didn't want it, like I tried for a few hours,

193
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:29,740
and I was like, I don't want to spend the time

194
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:31,380
to try and learn a new keyboard.

195
00:07:31,380 --> 00:07:35,220
So, I mean, so, again, of course,

196
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,220
the earlier story was that nobody wants to switch

197
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:39,620
because no one wants to have to carry around

198
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:42,380
their own kind of physical typewriter wherever they go.

199
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:44,860
But now, and going, once it turned out,

200
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:46,500
you could carry around your own little keyboard,

201
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,180
that's not that bad.

202
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:49,900
And then finally, once you're in a world

203
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:52,700
where you can just go and download the driver

204
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:54,740
and transform any keyboard into it,

205
00:07:54,740 --> 00:07:56,220
the fact that people aren't switching

206
00:07:56,220 --> 00:07:58,620
suggests, again, it probably isn't that.

207
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:01,740
At least it's not nearly as strong in effect

208
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:03,500
as human-like heavy belief.

209
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:06,180
All right.

210
00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:09,420
So, now, problems like this very naturally lead us

211
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:14,420
to another psychologically interesting,

212
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:16,500
the refinement of Nash equilibrium.

213
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,420
This one is not really formally modeled

214
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:19,620
because you really can't.

215
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:21,780
It really does come down to psychology.

216
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:25,140
But, however, a psychological idea discovered

217
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:27,220
by the great economist Thomas Schelling,

218
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:30,340
who just died very recently at his 90s.

219
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:31,900
So, you know, all economists are guaranteed

220
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:33,060
to live into their 90s.

221
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:34,980
So, all great ones.

222
00:08:34,980 --> 00:08:35,820
All right.

223
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:39,820
Yes, yes.

224
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:41,860
Hmm, well, no true Scotsman.

225
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:45,020
Yeah, he was great.

226
00:08:45,020 --> 00:08:47,580
All right, so anyway, Thomas Schelling

227
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:49,740
looked at problems like this and then he generalized them.

228
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:54,740
So, he came up with a very widely discussed example.

229
00:08:54,940 --> 00:08:56,820
And here's the example.

230
00:08:56,820 --> 00:08:57,980
All right, so suppose that tomorrow

231
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:00,380
you have to meet somebody in New York City.

232
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:02,140
You don't know the time or the place.

233
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:04,060
Where and when do you go?

234
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,300
Right, and he actually originally ran this

235
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:06,900
as an experiment where he had everyone in his class

236
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:09,820
write down what time you go to New York City

237
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:12,860
and write down where you go in New York City.

238
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:14,260
And I think over half the students

239
00:09:14,260 --> 00:09:15,980
would have actually met each other.

240
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:20,220
All right, now what time was the standard time

241
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:21,540
for people to go and say?

242
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:22,380
Noon.

243
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:25,340
Yes, noon was the most popular one.

244
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:27,340
I think the second most popular one was midnight.

245
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:28,620
All right, so noon or midnight.

246
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:29,900
And then where?

247
00:09:29,900 --> 00:09:30,740
Grand Central Station.

248
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:33,180
Yes, Grand Central Station, very popular one.

249
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:36,340
I think the other one was the top

250
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:37,900
of the Empire State Building.

251
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:39,420
So, there's a movie, Sleepless in Seattle,

252
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:40,260
where they meet at the top.

253
00:09:40,260 --> 00:09:43,820
So, I think the movie might have actually changed it.

254
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:46,820
Anyway, so the point of this example

255
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:50,540
is that there's an infinity of pure strategy

256
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:52,180
in Nash Equilibria for time and place

257
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:53,700
to meet tomorrow in New York City.

258
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:55,260
So, all you wanna do is meet the other person.

259
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:57,100
You just wanna be wherever they are at the time

260
00:09:57,100 --> 00:09:58,300
they're going to be there.

261
00:09:58,300 --> 00:09:59,860
So, it's just like this game except

262
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:02,220
with infinite number of equilibria.

263
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:04,060
Right, and if you go to anything,

264
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:06,460
any place other than the ones on the diagonal,

265
